LESSONS = [
    {"id": 1, "title": "What Is a Savings Rate?", "body": "Your savings rate is just the percentage of your income you actually keep, instead of spending. If you make $100 a week and save $20, your savings rate is 20%. This one statistic matters more than almost anything else in personal finance, because it's the clearest measure of whether your money habits are actually working. It shows you not how much you make, but how much you keep. Here's why it matters so much right now: recent research on U.S. teens found that only about 1 in 4 understand how to create and stick to a budget, and just around 4 in 10 teens are saving anything at all consistently. The gap really isn't about not having money, it's more about never being taught that your savings rate is something you can actually see, track, and improve. Try it: Pull up your own numbers in this app. What's your current savings rate? Is it above or below 10%?"},
    {"id": 2, "title": "How Compound Interest Works, For You and Against You", "body": "Compound interest is interest earned on interest. If you save $100 and it earns 5 percent interest a year, next year you don't just earn interest on the original $100, you earn it on $105. That gap grows every year, and it grows faster than most people realize. This works both ways. When you're saving, compound interest is your best friend and the earlier you start, the more time it has to snowball. When you're in debt, especially credit card debt, compound interest works against you the exact same way, and it adds on top of what you already owe. Here's why it matters so much right now: recent surveys found that roughly 7 in 10 young adults can't correctly calculate compound interest when asked directly. Try it: Look at your current savings in this app. If it grew by 5 percent every year with no extra deposits, roughly what would it look like in 10 years?"},
    {"id": 3, "title": "Needs vs. Wants: The Real Budgeting Skill", "body": "Budgeting isn't really about spreadsheets, it's mostly about being honest with yourself about what's a need and what's a want. Needs are things you can't reasonably live without, food, shelter, clothing, transportation, bills. Wants are things that are nice, fun, or social, but not required, eating out, subscriptions, new clothes when your old ones are fine. Neither category is bad. The problem is when the line between the two gets blurry, and wants start to eat up money that was supposed to go toward savings or actual needs. Here's why it matters so much right now: research on teen spending habits found that more than half of teens admit to impulse buying at least once a week, often triggered by social media. Try it: Look at your last week of spending. How much was needs, and how much was wants?"},
    {"id": 4, "title": "What Credit Actually Is (and Why It Follows You)", "body": "Credit is essentially trust, measured as a number. When you borrow money, a credit card, a car loan, eventually a mortgage, lenders look at your credit history to decide how much they trust you to pay it back. Credit isn't just about qualifying for a loan, it affects your interest rates, whether you can rent an apartment, sometimes even job applications. Here's why it matters so much right now: recent research found that a large majority of teens surveyed didn't understand the difference between a fixed and variable interest rate, one of the most basic credit concepts. Try it: Credit is built through small, boring, consistent habits, paying on time, not maxing out what you borrow, not through any one big decision."},
    {"id": 5, "title": "Emergency Funds: Why Just In Case Money Matters", "body": "An emergency fund is money set aside specifically for the unexpected, a car repair, a lost job, a medical bill. It's not for vacations or wants, it's a buffer between a bad surprise and a real financial crisis. You don't need thousands of dollars to start, even a small, separate pool of savings makes a real difference. Here's why it matters so much right now: people with very low financial literacy are roughly five times more likely to not have even one month's worth of emergency savings. Try it: If your car needed a $300 repair tomorrow, could you cover it without touching money you need for something else?"},
    {"id": 6, "title": "Banking Basics: Checking vs. Savings", "body": "A checking account is for everyday spending, it's what your debit card pulls from, and money moves in and out of it constantly. A savings account is meant to hold money you're not touching regularly, and it usually earns a small amount of interest, unlike most checking accounts. Here's why it matters so much right now: research on teen banking habits found that a large share of teens still rely mostly on cash, and many don't have a bank account set up at all yet. Try it: separating spending money from saving money into different accounts makes your savings rate, from Lesson 1, something that happens automatically."},
    {"id": 7, "title": "Understanding Inflation", "body": "Inflation means prices rise over time, so the same amount of money buys a little less each year. $20 today won't buy what $20 bought five years ago. This matters directly for saving: if your money sits somewhere earning 0% interest while inflation rises, you're technically losing value over time, even though the number in your account never goes down. Here's why it matters so much right now: research on teen financial knowledge found that well over half of U.S. teens don't fully understand how inflation affects their actual buying power. Try it: understanding inflation is what eventually connects saving to investing, money that just sits still can quietly lose value."},
    {"id": 8, "title": "Why Starting to Invest Early Beats Investing More Later", "body": "Investing means putting money into something, like a stock or index fund, with the goal of it growing over time. The single biggest advantage a teenager has over almost anyone else in personal finance is time. Thanks to compound interest, from Lesson 2, money invested at 18 has decades longer to grow than the same amount invested at 35, often resulting in a dramatically larger outcome. Here's why it matters so much right now: waiting to invest has a real, measurable cost, not just someday vagueness, but actual lost growth that can never be fully made up later. Try it: you don't have to invest right now, you just have to know it's out there, and that the earlier version of you always has the advantage."},
]